WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.787
When Autodetect misses a link, you take control.

2
00:00:03.787 --> 00:00:09.013
In this lesson, you will learn to create a relationship by hand, choose the

3
00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:14.240
right cardinality, such as one to many or one to one, and read the

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.107
relationship lines in Model view.

5
00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:17.600
Let's connect some tables!

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.220
Autodetect does not always find every link, so let's create one ourselves.

7
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.080
In Manage relationships, click New relationship.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.816
From table: Orders. To table: Order Details.

9
00:00:35.816 --> 00:00:39.838
Now click the column that links them in both tables.

10
00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:43.860
Here, it is Order ID, and Power BI highlights it.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.805
Power BI suggests one to many. Why?

12
00:00:57.805 --> 00:01:03.416
Each order appears once in Orders, but many times in Order Details, because one

13
00:01:03.416 --> 00:01:05.420
order can hold many products.

14
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.480
The cross filter direction is Single, so filters flow from Orders to Order Details.

15
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.220
And make sure this relationship is active.

16
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.053
The cardinality list has four options:

17
00:01:45.053 --> 00:01:49.502
many to one, one to one, one to many, and many to many.

18
00:01:49.502 --> 00:01:52.240
Always pick the one that matches your data.

19
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:58.618
Click Save.

20
00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.780
There it is, active in the list!

21
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.588
In Model view, a line now joins Orders and Order Details.

22
00:02:12.588 --> 00:02:17.480
The one and the star at each end tell you the cardinality at a glance.

23
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.251
Now, a one to one example.

24
00:02:39.251 --> 00:02:41.878
Here we have Customers and South Customers.

25
00:02:41.878 --> 00:02:44.880
Each customer appears only once in each table.

26
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.413
I link them on Customer ID, and Power BI sets one to one, with

27
00:03:02.413 --> 00:03:03.960
filters in both directions.

28
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.440
You just built two relationships by hand. Well done!

